{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789779397,"nonce":"CgXHYLEUX/mPikittn3zX7RMprs=","title":"Int64's own methods read `this.high`/`this.low` as object slots on the native i64","message":"`haxe.Int64` is mapped to the native i64 (`Int64_make`, `Int64_ofInt`,\nthe arithmetic ops, `get_high`/`get_low`), and the mapping comment says\nthe two-word stdlib implementation \"is never compiled\" — but it is: every\nabstract method NOT in the mapping (isNeg, divMod, toString/toStr,\nucompare, compare with mixed operands, parseString via Int64Helper) is\nlowered from Int64.hx, and those bodies read `this.high` / `this.low` as\nfields of `___Int64`, which lowered to `gep i32 this, [1]` on the i64.\n`Int64.make(0, 47).isNeg()` faulted at 47+8; `toStr` printed \"\"; the whole\nInt64 test family (TestInt64, Issue4842, Issue5493, TestDefaultArgs, and\nthe five rows whose import of haxe.Int64 failed on Linux) was behind it.\n\nFix, keeping the stdlib upstream-shaped: `___Int64`, `__Int64` and the\nabstract seen as a class (they share a root symbol) convert to I64;\n`new ___Int64(h, l)` lowers to `Int64_make`; a `high`/`low` read on any\nInt64-typed receiver (or a field owned by `haxe.___Int64`, for an inlined\ngetter body) lowers to `Int64_getHigh`/`Int64_getLow`; the constructor's\nword stores are dropped; `this` of an Int64 method is the i64.\n\nFound on the way:\n- `haxe.Int64.make(..)` (fully qualified) failed with \"Cannot find name\n  'haxe'\": the qualified type reference arm accepted only Class and Enum\n  symbols, not Abstract/Interface/TypeAlias (4 COMPILE_FAIL rows).\n- `Int32 + String` took Int32's `@:op(A + B) add(a:Int32, b:Int32)`: the\n  HIR lowering picked an @:op by operator alone. It now requires the\n  method's parameters to accept the operands (a String operand against a\n  non-String parameter leaves `+` as concatenation).\n\nStill failing in the family: Issue4842 (`isNull\u003cT\u003e` with T = Int64, a\n`Null\u003cT\u003e` of an i64), TestDefaultArgs (default-argument values), Issue10909\n(`haxe.Rest` array access), `import haxe.Int64.*` wildcard statics\n(TestInt64), `Int64.parseString` W0020.","files":null}]}